WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:03.320
The horror genre always seems to be evolving.

00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:05.020
Some of these changes happen slowly.

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:09.240
But occasionally a single movie rocks the
foundation of horror to such a degree that

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:10.730
the genre is never the same.

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:14.099
Here are some of the scare-fests that redefined
the horror genre.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:15.100
And our nightmares.

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:22.400
"Ha ha!"

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:25.480
A 
Nightmare on Elm Street

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:30.000
For a long time, horror movies were relatively
straightforward with a monster or, like, a

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.430
crazy person.

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:35.280
Then came A Nightmare on Elm Street, a film
that blurred the line between reality and

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:38.809
fantasy, creating a teen slasher with a supernatural
twist.

00:00:38.809 --> 00:00:41.930
In many ways, it was pretty much the first
of its kind.

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:45.850
What makes this concept so genius is that,
since Freddy Krueger played an otherworldly

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:51.469
role in the film, it left room to keep bringing
him back for sequels — lots of them, actually

00:00:51.469 --> 00:00:56.100
— to wreak havoc on teens in insane ways
that actually fit the story.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:00.559
Other franchises attempted the same supernatural
slant, but they just ended up creating a bunch

00:01:00.559 --> 00:01:04.610
of crazy guys with super-strength who never
seem to die, no matter how many times you

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:05.610
shoot them.

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:10.030
It really wasn't until Child's Play stuck
the soul of a psychopath into a doll's

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:15.170
body, that another film successfully blurred
the lines between reality and the supernatural.

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:19.890
But the knife-fingered man of our nightmares
went on to become an icon in the horror world,

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:24.799
showing it’s not easy to make a freaky psycho 
that can endure for decades.

00:01:24.799 --> 00:01:25.799
Jaws

00:01:25.799 --> 00:01:29.040
You might think Jaws isn't really a true horror
film.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:32.240
To that we say: go watch the beginning and
come back.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:33.600
Go ahead, we’ll wait.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:36.440
"AHHHH!"

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:44.860
Terrifying, right?

00:01:44.869 --> 00:01:50.130
The creature is basically unstoppable and,
with no rhyme or reason, starts slaying everyone

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:51.259
in its path.

00:01:51.259 --> 00:01:55.820
Jaws popularized the "giant monsters eating
people" genre for a new age.

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:59.810
If it weren't for Jaws, that genre either
wouldn't exist or, at the very least wouldn't

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:02.470
be nearly as vibrant as it is today.

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:06.460
While it wasn't the first film to do so, the
way that Jaws portrayed the terrifying creature

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:11.260
— almost always in shadows, seeming more
a malevolent force than anything that you

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:15.010
could actually defeat — would influence
movies for years to come.

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:19.430
After Jaws, came dozens of copies that were
basically just Jaws with a different animal.

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:21.840
A bunch of them were amazingly bad.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:22.920
Remember Anaconda?

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:31.220
Sorry — you do now.

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:35.120
Some were great, like Lake Placid, and some
of them were even made by Spielberg.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:39.400
But none of them would've existed if not for
that pesky, man-eating shark.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:40.870
The Blair Witch Project

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:45.160
When The Blair Witch Project hit theaters
in 1999, many moviegoers didn't know what

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:47.730
to make of the horrifyingly realistic film.

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:52.510
The movie's shaky camera work, documentary-style
production, and nose dripping close-ups were

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:54.340
elements no one had seen before.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:57.470
Plenty of people left theaters thinking the
footage was real.

00:02:57.470 --> 00:03:01.930
After Blair Witch popularized the found-footage
genre, many aspiring filmmakers applied the

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:03.739
idea to their own projects.

00:03:03.739 --> 00:03:07.659
Thanks to the relatively low budget needed
for filming, newcomers proved that practically

00:03:07.659 --> 00:03:11.490
anyone could make a movie using this technique
— though making a good one still proved

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:12.780
to be a challenge.

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:14.500
"Ahhhhh!"

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:21.120
As technology evolves so does this genre — smartphones,
GoPros, and security cameras allow found-footage

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:24.790
to provide different viewpoints, like what
we see in movies like Paranormal Activity

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:26.440
and Cloverfield.

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:30.280
Even the last season of American Horror Story
used found footage, setting up a house with

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:34.420
hidden cameras and locking people up with
just a smartphone so they could record their

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:36.180
own slayings.

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:37.530
The Shining

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:42.140
Stanley Kubrick's adaptation of Stephen King's
novel showed that you didn't need cheap scare

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:45.500
tactics and obnoxious music to make the audience
jump.

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:49.730
Instead, hotel scenes were kept well-lit,
and the ghosts were hallucinations caused

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:52.970
by Jack Torrance's cabin fever and creeping
insanity.

00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:56.880
Using savvy camera angles and intelligent
dialogue, The Shining redefined what it meant

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:58.060
to be a horror movie.

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:03.469
The hotel's creepy sense of isolation is emphasized
by the shockingly small number of scenes that

00:04:03.469 --> 00:04:06.019
feature all three members of Jack's family
together.

00:04:06.019 --> 00:04:10.689
Also, unlike horror movies before The Shining,
most of what could be classified as the scary

00:04:10.689 --> 00:04:15.569
stuff doesn't occur until the end of the movie,
while the rest is portrayed in a space blurred

00:04:15.569 --> 00:04:17.530
between reality and fantasy.

00:04:17.530 --> 00:04:19.660
The Texas Chain Saw Massacre

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:24.280
The movie where we first met Leatherface and
his favorite toy is notable for being one

00:04:24.289 --> 00:04:27.660
of the most realistic depictions of what it's
like to live in Texas.

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:29.560
No, not the crazy parts.

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:34.190
But some parts of Texas are terrifying all
on their own: those big empty wastes where

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:37.660
there's nothing around but fields, and then
a small house off in the distance.

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:41.800
It’s on roads like these that the heroes
of this film wander, finding horror as they

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:46.419
pick up a stray hitchhiker, only to realize
he's dangerously mad … and he's the nicest

00:04:46.419 --> 00:04:48.110
member of his family.

00:04:48.110 --> 00:04:51.039
Texas Chain Saw Massacre was amazing for its
time.

00:04:51.039 --> 00:04:54.520
It was one of the most brutal films ever made
when it came out.

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:59.310
And it defined what slashers would come to
be — savage, horrifying, and sometimes just

00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:01.800
a tiny bit inspired by real-life.

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:05.860
Despite a slew of successors, none have come
close to matching the ferocious horror let

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:10.720
loose by Leatherface who, oh yeah, is wearing
a mask made out of people's skin.

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:14.040
The first time you see him slaughter, and
the body twitches in detail?

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:18.060
That’s an image that'll stick with you,
whether you like it or not.

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:19.060
Alien

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:23.501
There had been sci-fi movies with giant monsters
before, but never a straight-up horror film

00:05:23.501 --> 00:05:24.501
about them.

00:05:24.501 --> 00:05:26.270
Then, Alien came along.

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:30.620
Not only did it bring about a renewal in body
horror across all science fiction, but it

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:34.530
also created the modern concept of science-fiction
horror films.

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:38.710
If it weren't for Alien, we would never have
gotten films like Event Horizon or Pandorum.

00:05:38.710 --> 00:05:43.210
But, being the excellent film it is, Alien
wasn't content with simply creating a genre

00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:46.600
— it also redefined how horror films operated.

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:50.830
Before this, final girls — the girls who
lasted until the end of the movie and occasionally

00:05:50.830 --> 00:05:52.710
won — were already a thing.

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:58.110
But with Alien came a bold new era of girls
who were as fearsome as the monsters themselves,

00:05:58.110 --> 00:06:01.960
changing how horror film protagonists worked
forever after.

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:02.960
Scream

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:07.380
This Wes Craven classic brought horror fans
back into theaters, after many had grown tired

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:10.900
of watching the never-ending sequels of '80s
slasher flicks.

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:14.509
Just how many times could Jason and Michael
Myers could possibly die?

00:06:14.509 --> 00:06:19.510
Scream introduced a fresh, post-modern take
on the genre, completely changing the formula

00:06:19.510 --> 00:06:21.060
for teen horror films.

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:25.130
Craven made sure his characters were familiar
with the tropes of those movies, too — knowledge

00:06:25.130 --> 00:06:27.380
that would be useful when their phones rang.

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:30.009
"For instance, number one.

00:06:30.009 --> 00:06:31.210
You can never have sex."

00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:32.979
"Booo!"

00:06:32.979 --> 00:06:37.060
Scream all but invented the self-referential,
self-aware horror movie.

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:40.910
Even the villain took time out of his bloody
rampage to make horror movie references, as

00:06:40.910 --> 00:06:45.120
he asks his victims trivia in order to decide
whether they live or die.

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:49.569
In order to stay alive, the teens think they
can use what they know from slasher films,

00:06:49.569 --> 00:06:53.949
creating a set of rules to follow like not
having sex, not doing drugs, and absolutely

00:06:53.949 --> 00:06:54.949
never saying:

00:06:54.949 --> 00:06:56.840
"I'll be right back!"

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:00.590
Paranormal Activity

00:07:00.590 --> 00:07:04.160
When it comes to Paranormal Activity, you
either love it, or hate it.

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:08.819
It was filmed with a handheld camera that
almost anyone could afford, in someone's house,

00:07:08.819 --> 00:07:11.090
with almost no special effects.

00:07:11.090 --> 00:07:16.040
There's almost no plot, no professional actors,
and no real big scares to be seen.

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:19.920
It's either a pretty great film, or the worst
film ever made, depending on who you talk

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:21.099
to.

00:07:21.099 --> 00:07:24.940
No matter what you think about it, though,
there's one undeniable fact — it helped

00:07:24.940 --> 00:07:28.979
usher in a huge surge of new found-footage
horror movies.

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:33.420
It wasn't the first, obviously, but with the
success of Paranormal Activity — a movie

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:38.680
made for next to no money — it showed movie
studios that people would gobble up found-footage.

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:42.910
It even helped keep the subgenere alive long
enough for a Blair Witch reboot.

00:07:42.910 --> 00:07:46.840
Thank Paranormal Activity — even if a whole
lotta people thought it was boring, it gave

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:49.320
us a ton of movies that weren't.

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:50.849
Night of the Living Dead

00:07:50.849 --> 00:07:54.940
One of the best movies ever made, Night of
the Living Dead completely free to watch because

00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:57.190
George Romero didn't copyright it.

00:07:57.190 --> 00:08:00.500
It's not his fault though — he wasn't an
experienced filmmaker at the time.

00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:04.540
The movie was his first, and it was basically
made with couch cushion change.

00:08:04.540 --> 00:08:08.720
Despite that , it became one of the most influential
horror films in history.

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:12.180
Not only did it bring about a renaissance
in independent filmmaking, it introduced the

00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:16.569
world to a classic horror staple: the slow,
flesh-eating zombies.

00:08:16.569 --> 00:08:20.680
Before Night of the Living Dead, most zombies
were magically controlled beings, finding

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:23.040
their roots in Western ideas of voodoo.

00:08:23.110 --> 00:08:24.860
But, Romero changed all of that.

00:08:24.879 --> 00:08:29.500
He introduced creatures that rose from the
dead, not due to any magic, but because of

00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:30.500
science.

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:34.400
While it's not explicitly said what exactly
caused the creatures to rise, all of the theories

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:36.920
concern NASA probes and viruses.

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:42.150
That's all stuff that exists in the real world,
turning zombies from fantasy monsters to science-fiction

00:08:42.150 --> 00:08:43.460
horror.

00:08:43.460 --> 00:08:44.580
The Amityville Horror

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:49.040
When it was first released in 1979, was released
in 1979, The Amityville Horror was already

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:52.850
familiar to the world thanks to the real-life
crimes that inspired the story.

00:08:52.850 --> 00:08:57.000
The film fleshed things out, giving viewers
more details about the infamous house — whether

00:08:57.000 --> 00:08:59.070
the details were all true or not.

00:08:59.070 --> 00:09:03.360
And it revolutionized the idea that a horror
film could be based on real-life events.

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:07.040
While other horror films have certainly claimed
to be based on true events, they're usually

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:11.000
so loosely based that the film hardly deserves
that subtitle.

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:14.880
The Amityville Horror, however, used news
reports about the events, interviewed police

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:19.760
who reported to the scene of the actual crimes,
and depicted the experiences of the Lutz family

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:22.710
as close as they could during filming.

00:09:22.710 --> 00:09:27.200
The success of the movie inspired several
other paranormal movies like Poltergeist,

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:31.321
and demonstrated how audiences are suckers
for horror flicks based on a true story, even

00:09:31.321 --> 00:09:34.170
if it may not actually be all that true.

00:09:34.170 --> 00:09:34.720
Saw and Hostel

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:38.560
Yeah, we're cheating and putting two movies
together, but it's only because they came

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:40.640
out at around the same time.

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:45.830
Before Saw and Hostel came out, horror movies
were very gore-filled, and very scary — but

00:09:45.830 --> 00:09:48.410
there were also typically plots and ideas.

00:09:48.410 --> 00:09:52.210
They were, you know, actual movies, not just
celebrations of gore.

00:09:52.210 --> 00:09:57.210
It wasn't until Saw and Hostel came around
that the idea of "torture-based horror" became

00:09:57.210 --> 00:09:58.720
an actual thing.

00:09:58.720 --> 00:10:02.880
It led to a slew of horror movies that don't
care about much outside of shock and awe — no

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:07.650
plot, no point, nothing but blood, guts, gore,
and torture.

00:10:07.650 --> 00:10:12.840
If it weren't for these films, horror movies
wouldn't be anywhere nearly as overtly brutal

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:13.900
and graphic.

00:10:13.900 --> 00:10:16.060
And what kind of world would that be?

00:10:16.060 --> 00:10:17.060
Thanks for watching!

00:10:17.060 --> 00:10:19.450
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:10:19.450 --> 00:10:20.801
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love too!

